name: finding files
boss: Globber
flavor: Name what you want. I will walk the whole tree.
syntax: {}, \;, !
brief: grep searches inside files. find searches FOR them. It walks a tree and
brief: tests every entry against predicates you chain together.
brief:
brief: Predicates are joined with an implied AND. -o makes it OR, ! negates.
brief: -exec runs a command per result; ending it with + batches many results
brief: into one invocation instead of forking per file.
brief:
brief: Quote your patterns. find . -name *.log lets the SHELL expand the glob
brief: before find ever sees it, which does something else entirely.
brief:
brief: the reverse reminder: -name '*.log' is a shell GLOB, not the regex you
brief: just learned in grep -- * here means "any run of characters", not "any
brief: number of the last thing".
---
q: %list% every file and directory under the current directory
a: find .
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find
hint: just find and a starting point -- no predicates means everything matches
---
q: %find% every file under here whose name ends in .log
a: find . -name '*.log'
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -name
hint: -name matches the glob against just the filename
---
q: %find% every .LOG or .log under here, ignoring case in the name
a: find . -iname '*.log'
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -iname
hint: i for case-insensitive, same idea as grep -i
---
q: %find% only regular files under here, no directories
a: find . -type f
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -type
hint: f is a regular file, d a directory, l a symlink
---
q: %find% only the directories under here
a: find . -type d
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -type
hint: d for directory, same flag family as -type f
---
q: %find% files under here but do not descend more than one level
a: find . -maxdepth 1
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -maxdepth
hint: -maxdepth caps how far down find will go
---
q: %find% entries under here at least 2 levels deep
a: find . -mindepth 2
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -mindepth
hint: -mindepth is the floor, -maxdepth is the ceiling
---
q: %find% files under here larger than 1 kilobyte
a: find . -type f -size +1k
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -size
hint: + means "more than", - means "less than", plain means "exactly"
---
q: %find% empty files or directories under here
a: find . -empty
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -empty
hint: matches files with zero bytes or directories with nothing in them
---
q: %find% files under here modified in the last day
a: find . -type f -mtime -1
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -mtime
hint: -1 means "less than one day ago"
---
q: %find% files under here modified in the last 30 minutes
a: find . -type f -mmin -30
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -mmin
hint: -mmin is -mtime's minutes cousin
---
q: %find% files under here that are executable
a: find . -type f -executable
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -executable
hint: tests the permission bits directly, not the file extension
---
q: %find% files under here with the exact permission mode 755
a: find . -perm 755
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -perm
hint: MODE alone means exactly this mode; -MODE means all these bits are set; +MODE means at least one is
---
q: %find% files under here with the setuid bit set, matching "at least these bits" rather than an exact mode
a: find . -perm -4000
out: suid-tool
ref: find
teach: find -perm
hint: a dash before the mode means all its bits must be set -- the file can have others too
---
q: %find% files under here owned by user @user@
a: find . -user @user@
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -user
hint: matches the file's owner, not who's running find
---
q: %find% files under here belonging to group @user@
a: find . -group @user@
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -group
hint: same idea as -user, but for the owning group
---
q: %find% either .log or .txt files under here in one command
a: find . -name '*.log' -o -name '*.txt'
out: \.(log|txt)
ref: find
teach: find -o
hint: -o is OR; without it, predicates are ANDed together
---
q: %find% files under here that are NOT .log files
a: find . -type f ! -name '*.log'
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -name
hint: ! negates whatever predicate follows it
---
q: %find% files whose path contains the word src
a: find . -path '*src*'
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -path
hint: -path matches the whole path, not just the filename like -name does
---
q: %find% .log files under here but do not descend into the logs directory
a: find . -name logs -prune -o -name '*.log' -print
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -prune
hint: -prune means "do not enter this one"; it pairs with -o -print
---
q: {stop at the first .log file found rather than walking the whole tree|find the first .log file and stop there, without walking the rest of the tree|find one .log file and quit immediately, not the whole tree}
a: find . -name '*.log' -print -quit
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -quit
hint: -quit stops the walk as soon as something matches
---
q: %find% every .log file and run wc -l on each one, one process per file
a: find . -name '*.log' -exec wc -l {} \;
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -exec
hint: {} is the filename, and the ; ends the command -- escape it from the shell
---
q: %find% every .log file and pass them all to one wc -l instead of one each
a: find . -name '*.log' -exec wc -l {} +
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -exec
hint: ending with + batches the results; ending with \; forks per file
---
q: %find% files under here without crossing into another filesystem
a: find . -xdev
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -xdev
hint: stops find from crossing into a mounted filesystem
---
q: %find% files under here following symlinks rather than listing them
a: find -L . -type f
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -L
hint: makes find follow symlinks and describe what they point to
---
q: %list% every file under here in depth-first order, contents before the directory
a: find . -depth
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -depth
hint: flips the walk order -- normally a directory prints before its contents
---
q: %find% every .log file and print the names separated by NUL rather than newline
a: find . -name '*.log' -print0
cmp: ref
ref: find
teach: find -print0
hint: this is what you pipe into xargs -0 when filenames may contain spaces
